+# AMENDMENT — panel review, 2026-10-05
+
+Written by a review seat, not by the proposal's author. It answers the four
+questions put to the panel, and where it disagrees it says so. Every figure has a
+producer in `scripts/` and reproduces on this checkout.
+
+## 1. The selection effect — first-pass-only is NOT sufficient, and it is not a common population
+
+Producer: `scripts/first_pass_rate_cannot_order_the_ladder_2026-10-05.py`.
+
+The proposal recommends measuring on first-pass falsifiers only, "which is a
+common population by construction". **That is refuted on the arm it matters on.**
+A model's first-pass findings are the ones IT chose to report, so first-pass-only
+does not remove selection — it replaces routing-induced selection with
+SELF-selection, and self-selection is the worse confound of the two because it
+rewards reporting shallow, easily-demonstrable defects. Measured on the real arm,
+372 first-pass falsifiers reaching a verdict across 5 models:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Kruskal–Wallis on first-pass severity | H = 31.1257, df = 4, **p = 2.886e-06** |
+
+Wolfram Language independently gives p = 2.8858595511049856e-06 for that
+statistic. The populations differ; "common by construction" does not hold.
+
+**And the statistic cannot order the panel even if the population were common.**
+First-pass falsifiers are written by a model for a defect it chose and already
+understands, so the rate sits on its ceiling:
+
+| model | k/n | rate | Wilson 95% |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| CC2 | 28/28 | **1.0000** | [0.8794, 1.0000] |
+| ChatGPT | 44/44 | **1.0000** | [0.9197, 1.0000] |
+| Codex | 59/59 | **1.0000** | [0.9389, 1.0000] |
+| Gemini | 164/172 | 0.9535 | [0.9109, 0.9762] |
+| DeepSeek | 64/69 | 0.9275 | [0.8413, 0.9687] |
+
+Three of five are at exactly 1.0000, so the top three rungs are **tied** — and
+saying which of them is asked first is the ladder's entire job. One rate is not
+rejected for the whole panel (χ² = 8.3057, df = 4, p = 0.0810; Wolfram
+independently 0.08099944746843583). Bootstrapping the induced ORDER with ties
+broken at random, as an implementation facing a tie would have to, B = 20000:
+**100.00% of resamples contain at least one exact tie**, 32 distinct orders
+appear, the modal order is reproduced **12.81%** of the time. A ladder keyed on
+this would reorder which model is asked to resolve the hardest findings on a
+coin-flip.
+
+**A better mitigation, and the measurement that says it is not available yet.**
+The estimand the ladder needs is conditional — P(resolve | the earlier rungs did
+not) — not marginal. A marginal rate equals it only if difficulty is independent
+of reaching the rung, which the ladder's own routing rule makes false by
+construction, so the bias does not shrink with n. The identified design is a
+paired comparison on shared findings with finding difficulty as a nuisance
+parameter (a conditional / Rasch fit), and routing already produces the overlap
+and archives it in `routing_history`. **It is not available from this archive:**
+of 144 findings carrying a `routing_history` and 175 recorded routing attempts,
+**0 come from the real arm** — the ladder has never produced one archived attempt
+on a real panel — and the simulated seat map is uniform, so the label pairs it
+does produce (`CC2 -> ChatGPT`, `ChatGPT -> Codex`, …) are pairs of **aliases of
+one model**. So neither mitigation is estimable today. The measurement has to be
+DESIGNED — a fixed residual set every model attempts, which is what Exp 42 did —
+and its precondition is a seat map with more than one model class in it.
+
+## 2. The provenance gate — NOT sufficient as written, and it failed in both directions
+
+Producer: `scripts/provenance_gate_misreads_the_mandated_form_2026-10-05.py`.
+Repair: `scripts/competence_provenance.py`, pinned by
+`bench/tests/test_provenance_gate_is_per_confirmation_2026-10-05.py`
+(16 tests; mutation-checked, 2 fail per mutation).
+
+A detached falsifier could pass the gate, and the compliant form could fail it.
+Two independent defects, both in the direction that inverts the ladder:
+
+**(a) The classifier was a substring match.** `re.search(r"open\s*\(|read_text|
+\.read\s*\(|linecache|getlines", code)` over the raw text. So
+`# could open(it) but I remember the numbers` scored as a reader, and so did
+`note = "did not open("`. Worse in the other direction: a code falsifier reaches
+its target by `import`, the form the CDSFL core directive REQUIRES and the form
+`bench/routing.py`'s own comment relies on, and `import bench.cdsfl_registry.engine`
+contains none of the five tokens. Measured over `bench/logs/`: **644 of 1034
+archived CONFIRMED falsifiers (62.3%) scored "detached" and do reach the target**,
+every one inspected by exactly that mandated import. Gating pool entry on the
+regex would have excluded the compliant majority and admitted the detached ones.
+
+**(b) The gate was per MODEL, not per CONFIRMATION.** The predicate was
+`confirmed > 0 and reads == 0`, so ONE genuine reader anywhere in a run licensed a
+numerator built entirely from detached confirmations. Demonstrated: 2 detached
+CONFIRMED plus 1 reading REFUTED **exited 0 and ranked that model first at 67%** —
+the exact inversion the ★ warning exists to detect. Note that the proposal's own
+numerator is per-confirmation, so the gate it nominates did not implement the
+quantity it was nominated for.
+
+Both are repaired. The residual is stated rather than closed: no static analysis
+can decide that the read EXECUTED or that the assertion depended on it, so a
+falsifier whose `open()` sits on a branch never taken still scores clean. The
+executing side already exists — `falsifier_verify.reverify_falsifier` runs the
+block — and recording which paths that run opened is the measurement that would
+close it. That is not done, and nothing should be read as if it were.
+
+## 3. Is the construct right? No — a continuous marginal rate is a worse instrument than the frozen measurement
+
+The ladder's decision is a ranking of **conditional** success probabilities:
+rung *k* is only ever consulted on findings rungs 1…*k*−1 failed. Exp 42 measured
+that directly, on a fixed residual set every model attempted — a small-*n*
+estimate of the right functional. A confirm rate measures the **marginal**
+P(demonstrate a defect) over a population each model selected for itself. Those
+coincide only under independence of difficulty and rung, which routing violates by
+design. So the proposed statistic is a **biased estimator of the wrong
+functional**, and more data does not help: §1 shows it is simultaneously at its
+ceiling and tied across the top three rungs on the real arm. The frozen
+measurement's weakness is its age and its *n*; the continuous rate's weakness is
+its estimand. Age is fixable by re-running the Exp-42 design. An estimand is not.
+
+**Recommendation: do not promote a confirm rate to the ladder, in shadow or
+otherwise.** Keep `DEFAULT_FALSIFIER_STRENGTH`. If the order is stale, re-run the
+Exp-42 design — a fixed residual set, every model attempts every item, provenance
+checked per confirmation by the repaired gate — on a seat map with more than one
+model class. That is one experiment, it measures the quantity the ladder uses, and
+it needs no new dimension, no Beta-Binomial prior, no minimum-sample rule and no
+shadow-ranking machinery. It is the simpler sufficient solution, and the proposal's
+own §4 shadow apparatus is more elaborate than the problem requires.
+
+## 4. What was missed
+
+* **`burst_mode="off"` in `bench/tools/run_simulated_experiment.py:480` is NOT
+  defensible, and it is a larger gap than the three config flags named.** The
+  whole burst block — *including the import of `bench/burst_planner.py`* — sits
+  under `if cfg.burst_mode != "off"` at `bench/reference_runner_v3.py:15231`. With
+  `"off"` the module is never imported, so `should_burst`, `plan_phases`,
+  `extract_signatures`, `build_phase_code`, `build_integration_code`,
+  `build_findings_summary` and both `*_convergence_overrides` have never executed
+  in any simulated run. `RunnerConfig`'s own default is `"auto"`, so this is an
+  explicit opt-out, and the parity comment above it itemises its neighbours and
+  not this one. Burst is the one mechanism the proposal cites as proof the
+  fingerprint is load-bearing, and the arm that rehearses the schema has never run
+  it.
+* **The live fingerprint's only behavioural consumer is `get_effective_context_budget`.**
+  See the correction inserted at the top of this document. The proposal's premise
+  overstates how load-bearing the structure it would extend actually is.
+* **`_load_fingerprints` swallows a corrupt profile** (`except (json.JSONDecodeError,
+  OSError): pass`), so a damaged file silently makes a model look cold-start. Under
+  the proposed minimum-sample rule that means "fall back to the frozen order"
+  without saying so, which is the benign direction but is unreported.
+* **The glossary question (open question 4) has a decidable answer.** The
+  implemented profile measures context capacity and productivity; `(D, v̄, A, C)`
+  describes something nobody wrote. Correct the glossary to what is implemented,
+  and record the verification score and coverage dimension as absent. Completing
+  the implementation to match a 4-tuple that no consumer reads would be an addition
+  nothing reaches — 11 confirmed instances of that class since 2026-08-01, against
+  0 removals of something needed.
+
+## What would refute this amendment
+
